================================================================================
Third-party data attribution
================================================================================

aml-filter screens names against the OFAC SDN (Specially Designated Nationals
and Blocked Persons) list.

    Specially Designated Nationals and Blocked Persons List (SDN List)
    Designated by:
             U.S. Department of the Treasury,
             Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC)
             https://ofac.treasury.gov
             https://sanctionslist.ofac.treasury.gov
    Records obtained from:
             Consolidated Screening List (CSL),
             U.S. Department of Commerce,
             International Trade Administration
             https://data.trade.gov/downloadable_consolidated_screening_list/v1/
             (only the rows whose `source` is
              "Specially Designated Nationals (SDN) - Treasury Department")
    Non-Latin aliases obtained from:
             OFAC's own SDN_ADVANCED.XML, retrieved through a public mirror
             (https://data.opensanctions.org/datasets/latest/us_ofac_sdn/),
             joined to the records above on the OFAC entity number.
             The bytes are Treasury's; the mirror is transport only.
    Status:  Work of the U.S. Government, in the public domain
             (not subject to copyright in the United States).

TWO SOURCES, TWO TRUST PROPERTIES — stated plainly because they are not the
same. Every RECORD (who is designated, and every field) comes from CSL, a U.S.
Government publication with no third party in the chain. Additional ALIAS
STRINGS in non-Latin scripts come from Treasury's own published XML retrieved
via a mirror, which is a weaker chain — so it is confined to aliases and can
never add, remove or alter a record.

Why the records come via CSL rather than OFAC's own export: since 2026-07-30
Treasury's sanctions-list service answers an automated request with either HTTP
403 or a JavaScript bot-challenge, so a build can no longer read it. The two
publications were cross-checked on 2026-07-30: 19,181 SDN records on each side,
with entity numbers agreeing 19,181/19,181.

Why the aliases are enriched at all: CSL publishes names in Latin script only,
and the matching engine preserves script rather than transliterating, so without
this a Cyrillic, Arabic or CJK query could never match. The enrichment adds
5,677 such aliases across 4,751 records (Cyrillic 3,675, Arabic 1,404, CJK 583,
Greek/Armenian/Hebrew/Georgian 15).

If the alias mirror is unreachable at build time the bundle is published with
CSL's Latin-script names only, and the build says so — a reduced-coverage bundle
is never presented as a complete one.

aml-filter does NOT bundle or redistribute the SDN list. The list is not
included in this repository; it is fetched at build time and ingested locally.
Always screen against the current official list — the SDN list changes
frequently, and a stale copy can miss or mis-match. OFAC's own publication
remains the authoritative reference for confirming any individual match.

================================================================================
Ported third-party code
================================================================================

The @amlfilter/browser package's `./engine` tier
(frontend/packages/amlfilter-browser/src/engine) is a TypeScript port of
edge-proc's browser bundle-sync engine — the signed, content-addressed
sync state machine, OPFS store, fail-closed ed25519/sha256 crypto, and the
canonical manifest/wire format.

edge-proc
    Source:  https://github.com/hseshadr/edge-proc
    License: MIT, Copyright (c) Harish Seshadri

The customer spreadsheet import/export workflow uses SheetJS Community Edition
for local XLS/XLSX parsing and generation.

    SheetJS Community Edition
    Source:  https://sheetjs.com
    License: Apache-2.0

